{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789228419,"nonce":"Wy0/1LlrqOAYZiRwoPqVTmL9AKs=","title":"Python frontend: gradual typing with a runtime object model","message":"Every Python expression gets a static type from a frontend inference\npass: int, float, bool, str, None, or object. `object` is a Python\nvalue the runtime owns (a PyObject behind an i64 handle). A local\nunifies over all its assignments; two different concrete types make\nit object, since `x = 1; x = 2.5` must still print `1` before the\nsecond assignment. Unannotated parameters are object; an unannotated\nreturn is the join of the function's return expressions, found by\niterating the module's signatures to a fixed point.\n\nCrossing from a static type into object is an explicit box call the\nfrontend emits ($Py$from_int, ...) and back is a checked unbox\n($Py$to_int, raising TypeError). The natives take scalars and handles\nonly; nothing depends on the compiler's Any coercions or the backend's\nsignature-driven boxing.\n\nLists, tuples, dicts, sets, class instances, functions as values,\nclosures (heap cells, so a returned closure cannot dangle), and\nexceptions are PyObjects with CPython's semantics implemented once in\nRust under crates/zyntax_python/src/runtime/. Generators ride on\nfibers. Module-level names become globals so `global` and class\nobjects work inside functions. Truthiness and and/or value semantics\nfollow the static type: a compare with zero when known, $Py$truthy\nwhen not.\n\nObjects are leaked until the collector lands (b94b4cb).\n\nMilestones, each a green conformance commit: typing pass with\ntruthiness and and/or; object runtime with list and tuple; str;\nglobals, closures, lambda, keyword arguments; dict and set; classes;\nexceptions; generators. Arbitrary-precision int (47a9c86) last.","files":null}]}